<!--
  /lifecycle-hooks — the mount(state, host) controller section + onCleanup.
  onMount does not exist; mount is a controller section and cleanup is onCleanup.
-->
<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Core concepts</p>
  <h1>Lifecycle</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Most of a component is declarative — signals in, DOM out. When you need to
    reach outside that model — an observer, a subscription, a third-party library
    — the controller's <code>mount</code> section is where it goes, and
    <code>onCleanup</code> is how it's undone.
  </p>

  <h2>mount</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mount(state, host)</code> runs once, after the element is in the DOM.
    <code>state</code> is the scope you returned from <code>scope()</code>;
    <code>host</code> is the element itself. Write a signal from here with plain
    assignment — <code>state.size = …</code> — and the proxy routes it through
    <code>signal.set</code> for you.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.mount}></sui-codeblock>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.template}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Cleanup</h2>
  <p>
    <code>onCleanup()</code> registers a teardown that runs when the component
    unmounts — disconnect the observer, close the socket, clear the timer.
    Everything you start in <code>mount</code> is undone here, so nothing leaks
    when the element leaves the page.
  </p>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Reach out from mount, not the template.</strong> The template stays a
    pure read of state; effects on the outside world live in <code>mount</code>
    and are always paired with an <code>onCleanup</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/control-flow">← Control flow</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/data-binding">Data binding →</a>
  </div>
</div>
